##
    What a field holds before anybody writes to it.

    A field of a primitive type starts at a zero of its own, and one of an optional type starts
    empty. Those are the only two: everything else has no such value, so the compiler asks for
    one where the field is declared or in a constructor, and says so if it does not get it —
    samples/negatives/compile/definite-assignment.pc holds that side.

    The two are not chosen arbitrarily. A number that starts at nought and a string that starts
    empty mean what a reader expects them to mean. A set or a function that started at "nothing"
    would be the null this language does without, reappearing in the one place the type system
    was meant to rule it out.
##

shared model Program

    # ---- Every primitive, left alone --------------------------------------------------

    integer counted;
    real measured;
    float estimated;
    boolean decided;
    character marked;
    string named;
    fraction part;

    # ---- An optional, left alone ------------------------------------------------------

    integer[]? gathered;

    function Main()
        Program.WhatAPrimitiveStartsAt();
        Program.WhatAnOptionalStartsAt();
        Program.WhatAnInstanceStartsAt();
    end function

    ##
        Each of these is the zero of its own type rather than a stand-in for one. A fraction is
        the one worth looking at twice: zero written as a fraction is '0|1', not '0|0', because
        a denominator of zero is not a number at all.
    ##
    function WhatAPrimitiveStartsAt()
        Console.WriteLine("counted:   " + Program.counted);
        Console.WriteLine("measured:  " + Program.measured);
        Console.WriteLine("estimated: " + Program.estimated);
        Console.WriteLine("decided:   " + Program.decided);
        Console.WriteLine("named:     [" + Program.named + "]");
        Console.WriteLine("part:      " + Program.part);
        Console.WriteLine("marked:    " + (Program.marked == '\0'));
    end function

    ##
        Empty is a value an optional holds, so an optional field needs nothing said about it —
        and the reader is made to prove it holds something before reading what is inside.
    ##
    function WhatAnOptionalStartsAt()
        Console.WriteLine("gathered:  " + Program.gathered.HasValue());
    end function

    ##
        An instance field answers to a constructor rather than to an initializer, and the same
        two exemptions apply: the primitive is left alone, and the set is asked for.
    ##
    function WhatAnInstanceStartsAt()
        Tally tally = new Tally();

        Console.WriteLine("tally:     " + tally.Report());
    end function

end model

##
    @summary: A count and the things counted, for showing what a constructor must settle.

    @remarks: 'seen' is a set and has no value of its own, so the constructor gives it one.
    'total' is a number and starts at nought, which is what a tally starting at nought means.
##
model Tally

    # @summary: How many have been counted.
    integer total;

    # @summary: The things counted so far.
    integer[] seen;

    # @summary: A tally of nothing yet.
    public function Tally()
        this.seen = {};
    end function

    # @summary: The tally as a line of text.
    # @yields: the total and how many were kept.
    public string function Report()
        yield this.total + " counted, " + this.seen.Count + " kept";
    end function

end model
